A||N||14||kornholio||Pulls when braking||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||03-26-2001||06:11 PM||||My car just had new front brake pads and calipers replaced.  When I brake, the steering wheel jiggles left and right and the car mostly pulls to the left.  What is causing this?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||03-26-2001||06:41 PM||||A pull in any direction usually means the opposite side caliper is hanging up and not doing it's job. Check the right caliper.||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||03-26-2001||06:46 PM||||If it's pulling to one side I would check to make sure whoever did the work remembered to lubricate the slider shafts on each caliper as this could cause the caliper to hang up and either not enguage evenly, or allow it to stick and prematurely wear out the pads. Also, if you had one of the calipers replaced it would be a good idea to have the other replaced since the new one could grab a little better than the other causing the car to pull to the side with the new caliper, or potentially blow out the seals on the old caliper (rare exception, but still there). ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||03-26-2001||06:47 PM||||Did you clean and lubricate the slider bolts that attach the caliper to the spindle?  If they don't slide easily then the caliper does not center itself over the rotor and only one brake pad is contacting the rotor.  This would cause reduced braking on that side and a consequent pull to the other side when braking.<P>The slider bolts can be pressed out of the caliper housing with a large channel lock plier and a small socket to drive it out of the hole.  The slider rides in two o-rings.<P>Use a Q-tip or a rag with some WD-40 on it to clean the bore and the 0-rings.  Then put a dab of lubricant (I use silicone grease) on the bolt and on each o-ring.  The slider should slip in with hand applied force and should slide back and forth pretty easily.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||03-26-2001||08:29 PM||||The rubber brake hoses can also be the problem. They have a nasty habit of getting blocked and making weird stuff happen.||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||03-26-2001||10:03 PM||||worst case scenario your A-arm bushings might be shot||||reg||1
Z||000006||jaguiler||03-26-2001||11:12 PM||||In order to clean the bore pick up a gun cleaning kit from Walmart.... 5- 10 dollars..<BR>Then use the brushes it comes with to clean the bore.... Hope this helps...I'm about to do mine as soon it warms up... (Black88GT) knows what I am talking about since we are from the same area.... brrr....<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>  |aguiler<BR>\/||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000007||kornholio||03-26-2001||11:20 PM||||Thanks for the replys.  I also had the ball joints, control arm bushings and flex line replaced.<P>I'm gonna try to grease up the sleeve that makes the caliper slide back and forth as soon as it gets warmer.  Or I might just bring it to one of those quick brake job places.  Any idea how much this would cost?||||reg||1
Z||000008||kornholio||03-27-2001||07:12 PM||||I pulled off the right front caliper today to grease up the slider shaft.  I didn't need to lubricate it.  They slid back and forth freely.<P>I did notice that there was no retaining clip holding the inboard brake pad to the caliper like it shows in the Haynes manual.  However, there is a little tab in the middle of the brake pad that prevents the pad from sliding completely off.  Would a missing retaining clip cause the pull?<P>I didn't take off the left side caliper to see if it had a retaining clip.<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||03-27-2001||09:07 PM||||Sounds like you have a bad set of pads.<BR>If a shop did your brakes, take it back and get new pads again. (should be FREE-defective parts)<BR>If you did the work, take the pads back to where you purchased them for another set.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||03-27-2001||09:13 PM||||Front<P>The outboard pad is held by a buterfly spring to the caliper.<P>the inboard pad has a clip that should fit tight inside the piston.<P>Make sure that the inboard pads are not binding in the bracket. If that pad is hanging against the bracket then you'll have these problems too.<P>After that... Mostly likely either your alignment is off or there is some kind of problem hydraulicly. Could be one of the new calipers is bad.<P>If you regrease anything make sure you use only silicone brake grease. oil based grease can cause problems.<P>The inside of the slider holes in the caliper should be coated with silicone grease all the way thru. The grease isn't just a lubricant. It prevents the caliper from corroding and binding on the slide. <P>There are other grease points as well. Those are ilustrated in the brake article in my cave. You only want a light film of grease on those other points. You are mainly trying to prevent corosion binding the caliper and inboard pad in the bracket.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||kornholio||03-27-2001||09:40 PM||||I had the car's front wheels aligned 2 weeks ago and it drives straight.<P>Is there anywhere I could buy just the retaining clip that I'm missing?  The inboard pad is just "sandwiched" between the rotor and the piston.  When I took off the caliper, the inboard pad just fell off.  There is nothing physically holding it onto the piston.<P>The reason I'm tackling this job myself is because the mechanic who did the work is 3 hours away from here.<P>I'll take a look at the other side's caliper tomorrow (if the weather's nice) to see if it's got the clip.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Indiana_resto_guy||03-27-2001||10:58 PM||||The clip in ? is 'stamped' to the back of the pad. If it is off or missing the pad will continue to shift position while applying the brakes.<BR>Get new pads.||||reg||1
Z||000013||kornholio||03-27-2001||11:25 PM||||The Haynes manual says that you may need to remove the clip from the old pad and transfer it to the new pad because some new pads don't include the clip.  So I was hoping that this part can be purchased separately.<BR>I don't want to spend money on new pads if I can just buy the clip.  It's the cheapest and easiest thing for me to do right now since the mechanic is so far away from me.  If I can't buy that clip from a store, I'll post a topic in the mall section.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Indiana_resto_guy||03-28-2001||07:57 AM||||If I were you, I'd take a trip down to the local parts store and see if they have a set of pads to look at. I feel that you will find the correct pads have a 3 pronged clip on the back which is 'stamped' to the pad. There are several inconsistancies in Haynes books that I have found in the past, I never noticed that one before now. That clip is used in most GM applications, but not in the Fiero.||||reg||1
